course: dictionary
lesson: 11736
title: A bristle on a seed
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An awn is a thin, stiff bristle. It grows from the seed head of some grasses and grains, like barley and wheat. Each awn is like a tiny whisker at the top of the plant.
explain: «The barley field was golden, and every seed head had tiny awns.» Tiny awns look like whiskers, so an awn must be a thin, stiff hair or bristle. Always ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Awns are sharp and stiff, so they can catch on things. «The fox ran through the field and came out with awns stuck in its fur.» Because awns catch on fur, they can carry seeds away from the plant. Awns can also get stuck in pets, so check your dog after a walk in long grass.
fact: An awn is a noun. One awn, two awns.
fact: «Awn» means a thin, stiff bristle on the seed head of some grasses and grains.
fact: Barley and wheat are grains that can grow awns on their seed heads.
fact: Awns can catch on animals' fur and help spread seeds.
fact: Grass awns can get stuck in dogs' ears and paws, so check your pet after a walk.
exercise: «The barley field was golden, and every seed head had tiny awns.» What is an awn? | *A thin, stiff bristle on a seed head | A sort of loud bird | A type of farm tractor | A large grey stone
exercise: «The dog came home with grass awns stuck in its ears.» Where did the awns get stuck? | *In its ears | On a bicycle | In a school bag | In the garden shed
exercise: Lily says awns are soft and bendy, like feathers. Is she right? | *No, awns are thin and stiff, like bristles | No, awns grow only on roses | Yes, awns are as big as trees | Yes, awns are soft like feathers
test: Which of these is a grain that can grow awns on its seed head? | Rose | Pebble | Oak | *Barley
test: «The seeds stuck to the sheep's wool because of the awns.» What do awns help the seeds do? | Float away in the sea | *Catch on fur and travel | Grow into trees | Turn into stone
exam: Tom walks his dog through long grass. Why should he check its ears and paws afterwards? | *Because awns from the grass can get stuck in them | Because awns make dogs sleepy | Because grass makes dogs taller | Because dogs need new collars
